# quirks.obj - a square pyramid, written as awkwardly as the format allows.
#
# Every line of this file is LEGAL OBJ, and every one of them breaks a parser
# that was written by looking at one well-behaved export. It is the test file
# for Lesson 3.5 SS8, and it is deliberately small enough to check by hand:
# 5 positions, 6 faces, one of which is a quad and one of which is degenerate.
#
# What is in here on purpose:
#   - CRLF line endings, because the file came from Windows
#   - a `v` line with three extra numbers (the vertex-colour extension)
#   - faces in three different corner formats: v, v//vn, and v/vt/vn
#   - NEGATIVE indices, which count backwards from the most recent element
#   - a 4-corner face that must be triangulated
#   - a face naming the same corner twice, which has no area
#   - keywords we skip (g, s, usemtl, mtllib) and one we have never heard of
#   - trailing whitespace, blank lines, and comments in the middle
#
# Authored for "Build a Professional 3D Game Engine". MIT.

mtllib nowhere.mtl
o pyramid

# The apex first, so the negative indices below have something to count back to.
v 0.0 1.0 0.0 0.9 0.4 0.2
v -1.0 0.0 -1.0
v 1.0 0.0 -1.0   
v 1.0 0.0 1.0
v -1.0 0.0 1.0

vt 0.0 0.0
vt 1.0 0.0
vt 0.5 1.0

vn 0.0 -1.0 0.0
vn 0.0 0.7071068 -0.7071068
vn 0.7071068 0.7071068 0.0
vn 0.0 0.7071068 0.7071068
vn -0.7071068 0.7071068 0.0

handedness right   # not a real OBJ keyword; counted as unknown, never fatal

g base
s off
# The base, as ONE quad with positions only: no uv, no normal, four corners.
f 2 3 4 5

g sides
usemtl none
# Position and normal, no texture coordinate. The double slash is required.
f 3//2 2//2 1//2
# The full form.
f 4/1/3 3/2/3 1/3/3
# NEGATIVE indices: -1 is the most recent position (v 5), -5 is the apex.
f -1//4 -2//4 -5//4
f 2 5 1 

# A face with a repeated corner. No area, no pixels; dropped and counted.
f 2 2 3
